**Vulnerability/Misconfiguration Link:** https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-102554 (CVE-2026-102554 — Google Guava: Denial of Service via unthrottled memory allocation during deserialization)

**Severity:** HIGH (DoS via excessive memory allocation; no reported RCE or data exposure)

**Summary:** Trivy flags Google Guava `33.7.1-jre` as vulnerable within the Jenkins Controller `jenkins.war` file. We have already upgraded to the latest available Jenkins WAR, but the vulnerability remains because the Jenkins distribution still bundles the affected Guava version. The vulnerability is fixed in Guava `33.7.2`, which has not been incorporated into the Jenkins WAR currently in use. This is an upstream dependency issue that cannot be resolved through a standard Jenkins WAR upgrade at this time.

**Ignore Reason:**

The vulnerable Google Guava library is bundled directly within the Jenkins WAR and is not independently managed by our Dockerfile or Jenkins plugin installation process. Despite upgrading Jenkins to the latest available release, Trivy continues to identify Guava `33.7.1-jre`. Manually replacing the embedded dependency could introduce compatibility issues or affect Jenkins functionality. We are temporarily ignoring this finding while monitoring Jenkins upstream for a release containing Guava `33.7.2` or newer. The exception will be removed once the patched dependency is available and verified.

**Risk Assessment:** Low (provisional). The vulnerability could result in denial of service through excessive memory consumption if affected deserialization functionality processes malicious input. Exploitation requires access to the vulnerable code path, and no such exposure has been confirmed in our Jenkins Controller environment. The vulnerability does not indicate remote code execution or direct data disclosure. Residual risk is being accepted temporarily while awaiting an upstream fix, with reassessment required if exploitability or exposure changes.

